The present study was designed to investigate the effects of the acute administration of the selective 5-HT~1A~ receptor agonist, 8-OH-DPAT, on the acquisition of cocaine-induced CPP in rats. The effects of the selective 5-HT~1A~ receptor antagonist, WAY-100635, on the acquisition of cocaine-induced CPP were also investigated.

Materials and Methods {#s2}
=====================

Animals {#s2-1}
-------

Male Wistar rats (200--250 g) were obtained from the animal house of the Faculty of Pharmacy, Cairo University. The animals were housed in groups of four per cage under standard laboratory conditions (12 h light/dark cycle, lights on at 7:00 am, temperature 22 ± 2°C, humidity 50 ± 10%) with free access to food and water. The animals were allowed to acclimatize to the laboratory environment for at least 1 week before the start of the experiments. All experiments were performed during the light phase of the cycle. All animal care and experimental procedures were conducted in accordance with the National Institutes of Health Guide for the Care and Use of Laboratory Animals (NIH Publications No. 8023, revised 1978) and were approved by the Institutional Animal Care and Use Committee of the Faculty of Pharmacy, Cairo University.

Drugs {#s2-2}
-----

Cocaine hydrochloride (Sigma-Aldrich, St. Louis, MO, USA) was dissolved in sterile 0.9% saline. 8-OH-DPAT (Sigma-Aldrich, St. Louis, MO, USA) was dissolved in sterile 0.9% saline. WAY-100635 (Sigma-Aldrich, St. Louis, MO, USA) was dissolved in sterile 0.9% saline.

